window.BOARD = {"rendered":"2026-11-26T19:15:03Z","prime_owner":"neve","types":["sketch","bug","enhancement","feature","research"],"areas":["board","config","ide","reed","fabric","selfreport","shuttle","burler","webster","stencil","loom","quarry","batten","shed","orch","creel","claude","codex","report","workspaces","deploy","dotnet","gate","testkit","pattern","shortcut","dobby","tender","hibernate","drift","darn","arbiter","landing","cleanup","termwindow","logger","git","cwd","friction","lenses","reconcile","preflight","parsers","process","fsutil","cli","pawl","lint","profiles","docs","heddle","tools","slack","weaver","testmap"],"sections":["Running","Layer A","Layer B","Layer C","Draft","Research","Enhancements","Features"],"projects":[{"handle":"P0","slug":"root","title":"Root: entries under no project","brief":"","labels":[],"parent":"","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P1","slug":"intake-wave-1","title":"Intake wave 1: PR modes, Webster, dotnet gate, reed, creel, commits","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P2","slug":"borrowed-orchestrator-ideas","title":"Ideas borrowed from comparable agent orchestrators","brief":"","labels":["feature","burler","shed","shuttle","orch","report"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-borrowed-orchestrator-ideas.md","done":false},{"handle":"P3","slug":"intake-wave-2","title":"Intake wave 2: Describe text, infra stops, push retries","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P5","slug":"intake-wave-3","title":"Intake wave 3: beam orch parent, scope and earlier checks","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P6","slug":"intake-motifs","title":"Intake motifs: run watch, landed records, closed area","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P7","slug":"closed-area","title":"A closed area per hub: Landlock and a seccomp connect supervisor","brief":"","labels":["feature","creel","cwd","reed","shuttle","claude","codex","dobby","slack","process","git","cli","docs","pattern","termwindow","hibernate","batten","ide","deploy"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-closed-area.md","done":false}],"entries":[{"handle":"T1","slug":"pr-modes-no-agent","title":"PR block in three modes, review, trail and off, with no agent under it","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","loom","config"],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P1","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Note `pr-ask-live-pane-test` is folded in only to be closed: it asks for a test of the parent-review ask this task removes.\n\nBundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# Take the parent out of PR-Review; the earlier reviewers must catch what it finds\n\nImported from [issue #223](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/223).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nToday the parent orch can be asked to review at PR-Review, and the operator has used that a lot.\nThe operator now judges it a bad idea, for two reasons.\n\nPR-Review is the very last gate, and it belongs to the human.\nWhen a run reaches it, everything the LLMs and LoomYard can do should already be done.\n\nA parent is also the wrong reviewer.\nAsking the parent, which is the orchestrator, to review a Discussion or code is work for the orchestrator, and it costs more than a cold-start agent.\nThe reviewers a run already has should catch what the parent catches.\n\n## Decision (operator, 2026-11-26)\n\nNo agent reviews the pull request any more: when the PR is made, the agents are done.\nWhat a parent finds when asked to review a PR must be found and fixed before the PR;\nthe checks that catch it are in the notes on reviewing the Describe text and on closing a task's scope at Code-Review.\n\n## Idea\n\nTake every agent out of PR-Review and add no seat in its place.\n\n- Remove the parent's ask and re-ask from PR-Review in `internal/landingshed` and `internal/loomcli`.\n- Remove PR-Review's own interactive session and its findings path: `pr_reviewer`, `lym loom pr-findings`, `pr_review_small_max`, `pr_review_ask_parent` and `pr_review_parent_timeout_min`.\n- The row keeps only the mechanics the PR mode needs (see the note on the PR mode's three settings).\n- Rely on the cold-start reviewers.\n  The `review-code` fan already holds `code-scope`, which traces every decision of the record to a change in the diff and flags a decision no change builds, besides `code-correctness`, `code-affected-set` and `code-tests`.\n  Read first which lenses the C# hubs run in `review-code` today, since the hub's own `lenses.yaml` and the C# layer can change the fan.\n- Decide what a run does with a commit pushed to the PR branch by hand while it waits.\n\n\n# A PR mode that registers the PR and approves it at once, with no agent under the block\n\nImported from [issue #224](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/224).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nThe operator often wants a pull request registered on GitHub without looking at it.\nWith a beam, say P1, and several tasks under it, the operator wants each task to land as a PR that is approved at once and merged into the beam.\nWhen all of P1 is done, one PR goes from P1 into its parent (P0 or root), and it lists every member PR.\nThat lets the operator backtrack and see how the work got where it is.\n\n## Decision (operator, 2026-11-26)\n\nThe PR block has three modes, and no LLM takes part in any of them:\n\n- Manual: the PR is made and waits for the operator's own review and approval, as PR-Review is meant today.\n- None: no PR; the row passes, as `pull_request` off does today.\n- Trail (`pull_request: trail`, operator 2026-11-26): the PR is made and merged at once, with no agent and no human, so the beam's PR history can be backtracked.\n\nHow the three modes are configured is not decided yet.\n\n## Idea\n\n- Trail: the PR is made, no agent is spawned, and Finalize merges it at once, as it does today after the operator's `lym loom pr-continue`; merging a PR without an approval has long worked in lym.\n- A beam's own PR lists its member PRs.\n  `Rewrite a beam's pull request description after each member landing` (`1cd11ee5e`) may already do part of this; read what it writes before adding more.\n- Manual waits for a human approval whatever the autonomy, which covers the note on autonomy and PR-Review.\n- Read first where `beam.yaml` `pull_request` is read (`internal/landingshed`, `internal/pullrequest`) and how a run reaches Finalize today when a PR is approved.\n\n\n# Autonomy: let PR review stay with the operator for one beam\n\nImported from [issue #217](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/217).\n\n`beam.yaml: autonomy` is one switch for both halts and PR-Review requests. A hub wants the orch to settle halts on the prime autonomously while pull requests into the prime's branch (main) still wait for the operator's approval. Today that needs autonomy off, which also sends every halt to the operator, or autonomy on with the orch holding PR-Review by hand. Proposal: a separate key, such as `autonomy_pr_review: false`, so PR-Review requests on that beam or the prime go to the operator while halts stay autonomous.\n\n## Issue comment, 2026-11-24\n\nSeen again, with a merge into the prime's branch:\n\n- The prime runs with `autonomy: true`, and the hub wants pull requests into main to wait for a human approval.\n- The task `import-project-names` reached PR-Review; the parent answered the parent-review ask `--satisfied` and told the PR-Review agent by creel that landing waits on the operator's approval.\n- `lym loom pr-continue` ran anyway about a minute later, and Finalize squash-merged the pull request into main once its gate passed, with no human approval on GitHub.\n\nA message to the PR-Review agent is no way to hold a landing under autonomy: the satisfied answer alone ends the review.\n\nExpected: a setting, per beam and for the prime, that keeps autonomy for halts but makes PR-Review wait for a human approval of the pull request (for example a GitHub approving review) before `pr-continue` or Finalize may merge.\n\n# No tmux-tier test sends parent-review ask from a live non-orch pane\n\n## Seen\n\n`failed-ask-hides-satisfied` (issue 213) left out its item 3: no test runs `lym loom parent-review ask` from a live non-orch reed strand pane.\nThat is the path that failed twice on 2026-11-23 in the mo hub (creel refused the LOOM signature), and the fix (708cdadc5) is pinned only below the tmux tier.\n\n## Idea\n\nA tmux-tier test: a built lym binary in a reed strand pane, a hub with a pull request record under autonomy, and a live orch strand that receives the post; assert the ask is delivered and the parent's answer lets `pr-continue` end the review.\n\n\n\n# README display\n\nThe board README shows each beam's pull-request setting (task `readme-beam-modes`); show the three modes there.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-23T18:56:36Z","revised":"2026-11-26T09:23:51Z","design_file":"design-pr-modes-no-agent.md","decision_file":"","revision":"8a861a4cec4bec96eeb46745f5044e95d2a1a6ac354ddf336cd9a9755470e751"},{"handle":"T4","slug":"dotnet-gate-fixes","title":"Gate test fixes for dotnet hubs: hung testhost, loggers, filters, tiers","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","dotnet","darn","burler"],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P1","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Bundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# Gate tier timeout_min does not stop a hung dotnet testhost; the slot stayed held 21 hours\n\nImported from [issue #216](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/216).\n\n## What happened\n\nIn hub `dop`, run `models-torque-drag-adapter`, a Webster batch fork ran `lym gate test --tier tier3` at 2026-11-23 15:36.\nTier3 has `timeout_min: 35` in the gate config.\nThe tier's `dotnet test` testhost hung (about 4.5 min CPU in 21 h), and the gate slot stayed held for nearly 21 hours: `lym gate status` showed the slot held since 15:36 the next day.\nThe 35-minute bound never fired, so the fork, Webster and the run waited without any stop or notice to the parent orch.\nAnother hub's orch noticed the idle process; the parent orch killed the testhost by hand, and the gate then released its slot.\n\n## Expected\n\nAt `timeout_min` the gate kills the tier's whole process tree (`sh -c dotnet test` → `dotnet` → `vstest` → testhost) and reports a timeout failure to its caller.\n\n## Likely area\n\nThe timeout may kill only the direct child (`sh`), or wait on output pipes that the orphaned testhost still holds open, so the wait never returns.\n\n\n## From issue #215\n\nImported from [issue #215](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/215).\n\nOn a Models hub, `lym gate test --wait --gate finalize` (PID 3536339) ran tier1, `dotnet test NORCE.Models.SlowTests -p:RunSlowTests=true` with `timeout_min: 30`, from 2026-11-23 15:36 to the next day, over 20 h. The test host had used about 7 min CPU; it was hung. The gate slot stayed held the whole time and the run sat at Finalize. On the same machine a DrillOpPlan hub's tier3 gate, started the same minute with a 35-minute bound, hung the same way and its bound never fired either. The machine may have been suspended overnight, but the bound did not fire before that either (15:36 + 30 min). Expected: the gate kills the tier's process tree at timeout_min and reports a timeout.\n\n# gate test: reports 'ran no test' when the detailed console logger's summary is used\n\nImported from [issue #235](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/235).\n\nWith `--logger \"console;verbosity=detailed\"` passed to `lym gate test`, the gate reported that it ran no test, although the test log shows `Total tests: 1, Passed: 1`. The gate's test counter appears to misread the detailed logger's summary format.\n\nThis is abnormal because a passing run is reported as a run of nothing, which an agent may read as a failure or retry needlessly. The counter should parse the summary lines of every console logger verbosity, or read the result from a structured logger (trx) instead.\n\nProvenance: task models-stick-slip-floor-tune, note Darn-Write.md.\n\n# gate test: tier3 command already carries --filter, so a second --filter in args makes dotnet test refuse\n\nImported from [issue #234](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/234).\n\nThe tier3 command of `lym gate test` already carries a `--filter`. Passing a test filter in `{args}` adds a second `--filter`, and `dotnet test` refuses the call. An agent found that `-p:VSTestTestCaseFilter=...` works as an override, but nothing in the verb's help says so.\n\nThis is abnormal because selecting one test class is the common use of the verb. The gate should merge a caller filter into the tier's filter (or replace it) and document the way to do it.\n\nProvenance: task models-stick-slip-floor-tune, note Darn-Write.md.\n\n# gate test: darn-write gate key runs tier0 only, so a tier3 measurement test runs nothing\n\nImported from [issue #233](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/233).\n\nA prompt told an agent to run a measurement test through `lym gate test --gate darn-write`. That gate key runs tier0 only, while the test lived in tier3 (a slow-test project, category `ModelsLong`). The first call ran no test, and the orch had to allow `--tier 3` by hand.\n\nThis is abnormal because the prompt and the gate key disagree on the tier. Either the prompt generator should pass `--tier` when the named test is not tier0, or the gate should say, when it runs no test, that the filter matches nothing at this tier and name the tiers that would match.\n\nProvenance: task models-stick-slip-floor-tune, note Darn-Write.md.\n\n# lym gate test answers that the hub declares no tier0, leaving a Burler fix round without a gate\n\nImported from [issue #237](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/237).\n\nA Burler code fix round ran `lym gate test ./\u003cmodule path\u003e` on a Go module and the verb answered that the hub declares no tier0.\nThe round therefore had no gate, and the agent ran `go vet` and `go test` on the module directly.\n\nThis is abnormal because the fix-round instructions end each round with the gate; a hub without a declared tier0 leaves the round with nothing to run, and the agent improvised a substitute.\n\nWhat to do: either have `lym gate test` fall back to a default tier when tier0 is undeclared, or make the refusal name the config key to add, and have the Burler code prompt say what to run when no gate exists.\n\nProvenance: task slug timesheet-suggest-tool; notes burler-code-r1.md.\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:38Z","revised":"2026-11-26T08:19:40Z","design_file":"design-dotnet-gate-fixes.md","decision_file":"","revision":"c6371e64fd4a5a04f38a7d91da452308db5a3934813825bcf932a5119e85ba8a"},{"handle":"T6","slug":"resume-restores-status-pane","title":"Loom status pane not restored after a reboot and a resumed run","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["loom","reed","tender"],"status":"running · Code-Burler-Fix","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #253](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/253).\n\nAfter the machine rebooted, a task run was resumed with 'lym loom resume' (a Go driver was spawned) and drove on through Drift-Verify, Code-Review, PR-Review and into Finalize. Its loom-status strand stayed dead the whole time ('lym reed list' shows it not live, and the task's tmux session holds only its shell window), so the operator saw no overview pane and took the run for stuck while Finalize ran the slow test tier. Another task resumed by its batten driver after the same reboot got its loom-status pane back. Expected: a resumed run brings its loom-status strand back, or 'lym reed list' or the run's status says the pane is gone and how to restore it.","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T12:30:41Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:31:36Z","design_file":"design-resume-restores-status-pane.md","decision_file":"","revision":"e918334c66a7bba54163f64e31bb348398583cd93c82ba075c5fef2d69ded9b2"},{"handle":"T19","slug":"drift-stale-loosened-ruling","title":"Drift-Verify reuses a stale loosened ruling after the moved checks land","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["drift"],"status":"running · Code-Burler-Fix","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #249](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/249).\n\n## What was seen\n\nA darn run deleted a test harness file (`Program.cs`) whose checks moved into a test class.\nThe drift judge ruled the deletion `loosened`, with three check groups `uncaused`, and Drift-Verify halted.\nThe parent recorded a decision to add the three missing checks and sent the run back to the writer, which committed them in a new commit.\nDrift-Verify then halted again with the same three `uncaused` checks.\n`_lym/drift/judged.json` still held the ruling from the first judge spawn, keyed on the diff hash of the deleted file, which had not changed.\nNo new judge spawn and no drift-judge parent ask came for the new commit.\n\n## Workaround\n\nA decision told the writer to commit an empty commit with one `Loosened-Check` trailer per check group, naming the commit that added the checks as the cause, then a goto to the writer row and a resume.\n\n## Expected\n\nWhen a later commit in the range touches the test files the stored ruling compares against, such as the class a deleted check moved to, Drift-Verify re-judges the loosened file instead of reusing the cached ruling.\nAt least, the halt's way forward should name the parent's ways out of a ruling that the code has since answered.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T12:30:56Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:31:36Z","design_file":"design-drift-stale-loosened-ruling.md","decision_file":"","revision":"0c147dbf5ff99c2243271be7134a7d053f08e0a2c7e490f7d9451ed4ea508c5f"},{"handle":"T20","slug":"guard-row-tooling","title":"Guard rows: an update mode for the settings golden, and a row that can state an exception","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["process","claude","gate"],"status":"running · Describe","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Part 1: an update mode for the Claude settings golden\n\nImported from [issue #257](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/257).\n\n### What happened\n\nThe Claude settings golden, `internal/shuttleengine/claudeengine/testdata/settings-golden.json`, has no update flag.\n`TestBuildSettings_MatchesGolden` only compares against it.\nEach change to a shellguard steer or pattern needed a temporary test file to render the new golden.\n\n### Why it is abnormal\n\nEvery guard-row change rewrites this golden, and each agent making one has to write and then remove its own renderer.\nThat costs turns, and a leftover or wrong renderer is a risk to the commit.\n\n### What to do\n\nAdd an update mode to the golden test that rewrites the file from the rendered settings and deny notice, reachable through `lym gate test`.\n\nProvenance: task `reed-client-and-bus-guard`, note `burler-code-r1.md`.\n\n## Part 2: a shellguard row can state an exception\n\nImported from [issue #256](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/256).\n\n### What happened\n\nA review finding asked for an exception in a shellguard row: refuse a command unless it carries a given flag.\nA `Row` in `internal/shellguard/rows.go` denies only when every one of its `Patterns` matches, and `grep -E` has no negation, so the row has no way to state the exception.\nThe first fix spelled \"any word but `--system`\" as one regex with quote and escape parsing.\nIt failed open on input it could not parse.\nThe fixer's self-check caught it, and a second version replaced it.\n\n### Why it is abnormal\n\nA guard row with an exception has to encode negation by hand in a regex, which is hard to read and review.\nA mistake in it can let a refused command through.\n\n### What to do\n\nGive `Row` a way to state an exception, such as patterns that must not match, rendered by each engine's guard builder.\nThe design has to keep the guard failing closed: an exception matched anywhere in the payload would let a second, refused command in the same call through.\nIf rows are to stay AND-only, make the hand-built helper (`unquotedWordOtherThan`) the documented way to write an exception.\n\nProvenance: task `reed-client-and-bus-guard`, note `burler-code-r1.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T12:30:26Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:38:07Z","design_file":"design-guard-row-tooling.md","decision_file":"","revision":"c21a91d6efbff7ae7e0cc7e00588ea8293219142014b5735c87107d2b2f4d36e"},{"handle":"T8","slug":"dobby-run-watch-build","title":"Dobby run watch and gate-hold notice: mail the parent on every stop and on a slot held too long","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["dobby","gate","batten","orch","pattern"],"status":"","project":"P6","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T4"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `dobby-run-watch`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record; the dobby tick's run steps and listedRuns (internal/dobbyengine/loop.go), the landing notice and its per-run memory (landnotice.go, state.go), the done-run clear (donerun.go), RunNoticeDeps.ReadChild, the per-task lookup over hubgeom.TaskSettingChain (tasksetting.go), orchcli.NotifyRunFrom and WithRunAutonomy, gateslot.Pool.Holders and LoadConfig, gatecli's runMarker, proc.TreeSample and PIDsWithEnv, pauserecord.AwakeSince\nDelivers: a run watch step in the dobby tick posting [run watch] lines per the run_watch level, with its per-run memory, owed lines and gate-hold keys in a new watch map of the dobby state record; beam.run_watch as a per-task key with its check; a probe-tick gate-hold check with gate_hold_factor and gate_hold_min hub keys and its [dobby notice] line; the tier run's environment marker in the slot holder record; orch-role Notices, dobby doc and template, and PATTERN-config-layers naming the new lines and keys\nExports: dobbyengine.LookupRunWatch, beamsetting's RunWatchKey and its three values, the dobby state record's watch map, gateslot.Holder's marker field\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 750 changed lines)\n- internal/beamsetting (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/beamcli (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/gateslot (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/gatecli (about 30 changed lines)\n- contracts (about 20 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 5 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T11:22:01Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:22:01Z","design_file":"design-dobby-run-watch-build.md","decision_file":"decision-dobby-run-watch-build.md","revision":"27ffb7923c6a6986a02b7462e261c649597d71a5cfcc6504ee387bd68187293d"},{"handle":"T9","slug":"describe-review-and-sync","title":"Review the Describe text in its row, keep LoomYard terms out, sync the PR body","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","stencil","burler","shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T1"],"brief":"","body":"This task builds items 1 and 2 of the Describe-text note's Idea; items 3 to 6 belong to the task that closes scope at Code-Review.\nNo agent reviews the pull request any more (task `pr-modes-no-agent`), so the Describe text is the last text an LLM checks.\n\nBundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# The Describe text names no LoomYard terms, except the Created using block\n\nImported from [issue #227](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/227).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nAn analysis of the parent's PR-Review findings in the C# hubs found that about 12 findings were internal process or tool names in the PR text: \"item 3 of the task\", \"the test scoper cannot run C# tests there\", \"the task's branch point\", \"The board entry proposed\", \"The parent recorded...\".\nThe stencil that writes the description (`landing-template-describe`) does not forbid them, so the parent had to tell the Describe session so in each hub.\nIn one run the parent noted its own earlier instruction had caused it.\n\nThe operator's rule: LoomYard, and anything from it, does not belong in the Describe text.\nThe one exception is the \"Created using\" block at the bottom.\n\n## Idea\n\nKeep LoomYard's vocabulary out of the description the Describe row writes, and let lym hold it.\n\n- Add the rule to the Describe stencil: the description names no board, task, Sketch, decision record, parent, orch, scoper, gate, tier, perch or lym term, and no \"item N of the task\".\n- Let the Describe row's mechanical gate (`NewDescriptionGate`, which runs `summaryparser.ValidateDescription`) refuse such a term outside the credit block, with the refusal naming the term and the line.\n- Leave the credit block as it is: the `credit` keys in `landing.yaml` append it, and the term check skips it.\n- Read first how the credit block is appended to the description, and which words the check can match without refusing a repository's own legitimate names.\n- The issue on reviewing the Describe text covers claims and missing content; this issue covers the vocabulary.\n\n\n# Review the Describe text inside the Describe row, and catch the rest of what the parent finds at PR-Review earlier\n\nImported from [issue #225](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/225).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nIn the C# repos on one machine, a parent asked to review a PR always finds something.\nThat points to a design flaw: if the parent finds something, an earlier quality check missed it.\n\nThe operator's guess was that the delivered code often does not cover everything the Discussion's decision record asked for.\nThe operator judges the finding below, that most of it is the PR text, as not very important, and that it is solved by reviewing the Describe text too.\n\n## What the parent finds (analysis, 2026-11-24)\n\nAn analysis read 25 PR-Review rounds with findings, across four hubs; one more hub had no PR-Review records.\nAbout 65 findings are itemised in readable text; about 29 more were in scratchpad files that no longer exist.\nThe task worktrees and the Sketches of the landed tasks are gone, so the only full source is each hub's creel mail journal, and the gate attributions are mostly inferred.\nThe full report is a local file and is not committed.\n\n- About half of the findings (32 of 65) are about the PR description, not the code:\n  internal process or tool names in the text (12), wrong or overreaching claims (12), and content the task asked for that the text omits (8).\n- The rest are tests missing or too costly (10), wrong behaviour (8), doc or comment drift (7) and scope the Sketch did not hold (8).\n- The operator's guess holds only in part: 4 of 25 rounds cite a Sketch or task requirement that was missed, and all four concern the PR text or scope, not code behaviour.\n- A finding is often sent twice because the agent edits the summary file but not the GitHub PR body (about 5 rounds).\n\n## Idea\n\nThe Describe row writes the change description, which becomes the PR title, the PR body and the landing commit message.\nIt is a seat table (`DescribeTable` in `internal/landingshed/describe.go`) whose session already reads the decision record, and its only gate is mechanical (`ValidateDescription`); no agent reviews what the text says.\n\n1. Review the Describe text inside the Describe row with a second agent, an assistant seat, and send its findings back to the writer; Describe is the last step that uses an LLM, so no review runs after it.\n   The reviewer checks that the text names no internal process or tool (board, task, scoper, Sketch, decision record, \"item N of\"), that every claim holds against the diff and the task's report or measurements, and that every deliverable the Sketch says to state in the description is there.\n   The mechanical gate stays in front of it.\n   This covers the 32 findings about the text.\n2. Sync the GitHub PR body from the description on every push, and verify the two match (about 5 re-sent rounds).\n3. Run the mutation or redundancy tier on every C# task, and give the Plan a tier0 cost budget (10 test findings).\n4. Have Discussion record what the parent will check: measured figures, doc statements and test intent.\n5. Let a Code-Review finding the parent already ruled in scope reach a fixer; today a decision finding spawns none.\n6. Re-check a constant quoted in a doc when the case it was measured on changes.\n\nThe issue on taking the parent out of PR-Review leaves these checks to the cold-start reviewers, so they must run before PR-Review, and a task's scope closes at its first Code-Review round (see the issue on closing a task's scope).\n\n\n# The GitHub PR body is not updated when the description file changes\n\nImported from [issue #228](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/228).\n\n## Seen\n\nAn analysis of the parent's PR-Review findings in the C# hubs found that the same findings were sent again in about five rounds.\nThe agent fixed the description file but the pull request's body on GitHub was not updated, so the parent read the old text again.\nSeen in five separate rounds across several runs.\n\n## Why it makes no sense\n\nThe description file is the one source of the PR title and body.\nA PR whose body differs from the file the run holds is wrong from the first push, and a review round is spent on text that has already been fixed.\n\n## Idea\n\nMake the PR body follow the description file on every push, and refuse to end a round while the two differ.\n\n- Read first where `pr update` runs for a task's pull request, and which path writes the description file without calling it (`lym beam describe` runs the `pr update` sequence for a beam; check the task path).\n- After each push, compare the GitHub PR body with the file, and update it or halt with a way forward naming the difference.\n- Add a test that edits the description file after the PR exists and expects the body to follow on the next push.\n\n\n# Describe halts failed on an uninformative shuttle run outcome timeout\n\nImported from [issue #244](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/244).\n\nThe Describe step halted the run as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure` and the message `shedadapters: Describe (seats): shuttle run outcome timeout`.\n\nWhy it is abnormal:\n- The note carries no detail on which timeout elapsed, how long the shuttle run had been going, or whether the agent was still working.\n- A timeout on a seated shuttle run is classed as a hard failure, so the run stopped as `failed` rather than being resumed or escalated.\n- The next attempt of the same step completed (`Describe / done`) about 34 minutes later, which suggests the timeout was not a defect in the producer's work.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Include the elapsed time, the timeout limit and the shuttle run's last known state in the halt reason.\n- Decide whether a shuttle outcome timeout should be a retryable or `blocked` stop instead of `failed`.\n\nProvenance: task `retire-d9`; note loom-halt.md.\n\n\n# Describe seat waits out the full timeout after an operator interrupt, then halts as hard failure\n\nImported from [issue #241](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/241).\n\nThe Describe producer (seats engine) halted the run as `failed` with `shuttle run outcome timeout`, after its agent session sat held for about 28 minutes with no output.\n\nSequence from the trace:\n\n```\n22:15:05 shuttle: run started role=describe\n22:16:51 shuttle: session state state=idle-stalled cause=interrupt\n22:16:53 shuttle: session state state=busy cause=turn\n22:16:57 shuttle: turn end held, output files missing\n22:16:57 shuttle: held turn end of a turn the operator started; no hold notice\n22:16:57 shuttle: session state state=idle-stalled cause=no-output loop=held\n22:45:06 shuttle: run finished outcome=timeout cleanedUp=false\n22:45:29 shed: step refused kind=producer transient=\"\" error=\"shedadapters: Describe (seats): shuttle run outcome timeout\"\n```\n\nThe halt note reads `loom halted failed at Describe`, `anomaly: producer-hard-failure`, 12 history entries.\n\nWhy this is abnormal:\n- The operator interrupted the agent once and typed into the pane, which made the turn \"operator-started\".\n  lym then held the turn end without a hold notice and waited silently until the run timeout.\n- The session was `idle-stalled` for the whole wait, and nothing re-prompted the agent for its missing output files or surfaced the state to the operator.\n- The timeout was then recorded as a hard failure with `transient=\"\"`, although the cause was an interrupted session and not a producer defect.\n- `cleanedUp=false` on the finished run, and the strand was stopped with `live=true` afterwards.\n\nSuggested fix: when a turn end is held for missing output files after an operator-started turn, send the agent a reminder or hand the pane back to the operator with a visible notice instead of waiting out the whole timeout.\nClassify a timeout that follows an operator interrupt as resumable, not as `producer-hard-failure`.\n\nProvenance: task `transient-tripout-overpull`, from `loom-halt.md`.\n\n\n\n\n## From issue #255\n\nImported from [issue #255](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/255).\n\nThe Describe step wrote a pull request body that names lym, LoomYard and another task, and the run's docs carried the same kind of internal names.\nCode-Review approved the diff, and PR-Review raised the leak afterwards as a send-back with three findings: one BLOCKING (the pull request body names lym internals), one MEDIUM (two document sections describe lym internals), one NIT.\nThe same class of leak had already been fixed by hand-written commits earlier in the run, such as a rewrite of a document to describe test tiers by what they run instead of through the gate.\n\nThis is abnormal because the output goes into a repository that does not know lym: a pull request body and repository documents should not name lym, LoomYard or other tasks.\nNeither the Describe nor the Code-Review instructions caught it, so the leak cost a PR-Review send-back and a second Code-Bouncer, Code-Burler-Review and Code-Burler-Fix cycle.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Tell the Describe step, and any step that writes repository documents, not to name lym, LoomYard, gates, or other tasks in a pull request body or a repository file.\n- Have Code-Review check documents and the pull request body for such names, so the finding comes before approval and not from PR-Review.\n\nProvenance: task `dead-code-and-timing-docs`; notes `pr-review-send-back-1.md`, `loom-halt.md` (history only).\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:13Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:30:33Z","design_file":"design-describe-review-and-sync.md","decision_file":"","revision":"9f638be020ff0883b24457ac725895ac6e03ef91880799ca9110694d34e0ed09"},{"handle":"T10","slug":"infra-stops-not-failures","title":"Machine stops, dead judges and idle seats are not producer failures","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["shuttle","claude","tender","gate","burler","shed","reed"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T4"],"brief":"","body":"Bundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# Gate fixer interrupted by a machine stop is reported as dead (timeout)\n\nImported from [issue #248](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/248).\n\n## What happened\n\nA row gate spawned a cold fixer, and the machine rebooted under a minute later.\nThe run was re-stepped more than two days after that.\nThe loop's reconcile had stopped the fixer's shuttle run, but the gate fixer's record still read live.\nThe restarted driver waited on that record, classified it `dead` with reason `timeout`, and told the writer:\n\n\u003e The cold fixer ended without a reply (timeout); any change it left uncommitted is in the worktree.\n\nThe interrupted-stop detail, from the first entry after the reboot:\n\n```\nloop exit: not recorded, since no process marked the loop's exit, and the machine has booted since the loop started, so a stop of the machine likely ended it; (...) stopped shuttle runs: ly:\u003cslug\u003e:gate-fixer; left runs whose output files all exist: ly:\u003cslug\u003e:darn\n```\n\nThe fixer's shuttle `run.json` (excerpt):\n\n```\n\"createdAt\": \"2026-11-23T19:19:20Z\",\n\"outcome\": \"stopped\",\n\"roleKey\": \"gate-fixer\",\n\"interrupted\": true\n```\n\nThe gate fixer's `records.json` after the resume (excerpt):\n\n```\n\"spawnedAt\": \"2026-11-23T19:19:27Z\",\n\"status\": \"dead\",\n\"deadReason\": \"timeout\"\n```\n\n## Why it is abnormal\n\nThe fixer did not run out of time: the machine stop ended it, and lym had already recorded its run as interrupted.\n`Await` in `internal/gatefixer/await.go` checks the elapsed time since spawn before it checks whether the strand is live,\nso any fixer interrupted by a stop that outlasts its timeout is reported as `timeout` instead of `died`.\nThe writer and anyone reading the record are sent looking for a slow fixer or a timeout that is too short.\n\nThe dead record also stays in the visit's set, which `internal/gatefixer/rowgate.go` counts against the fixer budget.\nThe interrupted fixer's session is not resumed, so the failure it was given falls back to the writer.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Report a fixer whose run is marked interrupted, or whose strand is gone across a reboot, as interrupted or `died`, not `timeout`.\n- Consider resuming its session, or spawning a replacement that does not use up the budget, as is done for interrupted run agents.\n\nProvenance: task `sandbox-without-new-kernel`, note `loom-halt.md`.\n\n\n# A died bouncer judge session spends Code-Bouncer's bounce budget\n\nImported from [issue #239](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/239).\n\nOn a flaky network, Code-Bouncer's judge session twice failed to start: shuttle logged 'provider never became ready; strand torn down', relaunched, and the second run finished at once with no transcript. shedadapters then logged 'bouncer judge run did not complete ... outcome=died', and the row stopped with 'bounce budget exhausted for Code-Bouncer'. The judge never gave a verdict, so no bounce happened, yet the run stalled until a manual `lym loom resume`.\n\nExpected: a judge that dies before giving a verdict is relaunched, or the row halts as an infrastructure failure, without spending the bounce budget meant for real verdicts.\n\n# tender revive reads Claude Code placeholder after an API error as a typed draft and skips the session\n\n## Seen\n\nOn 2026-11-26 two Claude sessions stalled on `API Error: Connection refused (ECONNREFUSED)`: `ly:rc-click-wrong-client-session:darn` and `ly:async-run-pusher:code-burler-fix`.\n`lym tender revive` skipped both with reason `the input box holds a draft: \"Resend your message to try again\"`.\nThat text is Claude Code's own prompt after an API error, not text anyone typed, so revive never reaches the case it exists for.\nNo code on `origin/main` names the string.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe orch paused each run with `lym batten pause \u003cslug\u003e` and resumed it with `lym batten run \u003cslug\u003e`, which relaunches the row's agent on its own session.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe input-box reader in `internal/shuttleengine/claudeengine` treats Claude Code's post-error prompt as an empty box, and `tender revive` gives the session its carry-on line.\n\n\n# Shuttle idle check reports no input box on an idle seat, killing Plan-Burler-Review\n\nImported from [issue #219](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/219).\n\n## What happened\n\nThe `Plan-Burler-Review` row (`burler-review`) halted `failed` (anomaly `producer-hard-failure`) after round 3: two consecutive `died` outcomes, both \"review never started\".\nEach attempt's seat start ended with:\n\n```\nshuttle: deliver the prompt after loading skills: shuttle: the session is not idle: strand \"\u003cid\u003e\": no input box is on screen; a menu or picker may be open; retry once the session is idle.\n```\n\nThe pane lines quoted in the same error show the opposite: the skill-load reply `ok scribe:prose, scribe:code-quality, scribe:testing` had finished, a `done` status line was printed, and the `❯` input prompt plus the `bypass permissions on` footer were on screen.\nThe session was idle with an input box; the idle check reported none.\nIt happened on attempts 3 and 3b, in two separate halts, with the same pane text each time (`Sautéed for 3s`, `Cogitated for 3s`, `Cooked for 2s` status lines).\n\n## Why abnormal\n\nThe seat was healthy.\nA false \"no input box\" reading kills a seat that never got its prompt, burns the review rounds, and halts the whole run as `failed`.\nThe error text itself says \"retry once the session is idle\", yet the shuttle did not retry after the capture showed an idle pane.\n\n## What to do\n\nCheck the input-box detection in the shuttle's deliver-after-skills step against a pane whose last lines are a completed skill-load reply, a `✻ \u003cverb\u003e for Ns · done` line, the `❯` prompt and the permissions footer.\nRetry the idle check for a short window before declaring `died`, so a repaint race right after the skill-load reply does not kill the seat.\n\nProvenance: task `models-torque-drag-adapter`; notes `loom-halt-2.md`, `loom-halt-3.md`.\n\n\n# Review findings carried over from two landings\n\n## Strand pane memory cap (`10ae7e112`)\n\n- The degraded-launch Warn, on a host without the memory controller delegated to the user manager, has no test of its own.\n- The OOM notice can name an earlier kill, or the wrong process, when several panes share a cgroup or the journal line lands after the kill is counted; a shared cgroup is only partly covered.\n- The notice's delivery to the parent orch is untested end to end.\n- The OOM step reads the strands, which waits on the operation lock; check that it does not stall the watchdog loop.\n\n## Resume after a crash (`dfade5f9a`)\n\n- `removeSupersededStrands` (`internal/shuttleengine/attach.go`) records a superseded running strand's stop as interrupted; check a test covers that path.\n- The start retry loop (`internal/shuttleengine/run.go`) reuses the resume on a retried start; a resumed start that fails to come up should retry fresh.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T07:25:04Z","revised":"2026-11-26T08:20:22Z","design_file":"design-infra-stops-not-failures.md","decision_file":"","revision":"b7839ecd905709ea8d5085421aafb30d6afcca5570baeb4cbfa2865c9d2d78e0"},{"handle":"T18","slug":"reach-misses-tier1-calls","title":"gate reach: functions called directly by tier1 tests are reported as reached by no mapped test","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","testmap"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T4"],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #250](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/250).\n\n## What happened\n\nDuring a code review fix round, the reach note listed `hubCancelDeps`, `Topology.SettleGonePairTeardown` and `Topology.RemoveLocal` as reached by no mapped test.\nEach is called directly by a tier1 test.\nThe maps of both packages belonged to the worktree itself and were minutes old.\n`lym gate reach` gave the same answer.\nThe note carried the caveat \"may run it unseen\".\n\n## Why it is abnormal\n\nA function that a tier1 test calls directly should be mapped to that test, above all with a fresh map of the worktree's own.\nThe false \"unreached\" answer could only be ruled out by reading the tests and running them.\nThe caveat does not say why the map missed these tests, so the reader cannot tell a map defect from a real coverage gap.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Find why the reach map misses direct calls from tier1 tests, and fix the mapping.\n- Where the map cannot see a call, have the caveat name the reason for that function.\n\nProvenance: task `remove-record-rerun-pair-gone`, note `burler-code-r2.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T12:30:52Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:31:37Z","design_file":"design-reach-misses-tier1-calls.md","decision_file":"","revision":"7ce6cc2003f4dad048793d376c1cd061b8c35856d4b2ba6deeacbf9506aa825b"},{"handle":"T11","slug":"push-retry-not-halt","title":"Background pushes retry quietly; landing pushes and reads retry instead of failing","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["fabric","shed","landing","batten","dobby"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer B","waits_on":["T1","T9","T10"],"brief":"","body":"## Decisions (operator, 2026-11-26)\n\n- No mail per failed push. lym retries quietly with backoff.\n  One summary notice goes to the orch when owed work has stayed unpushed past a threshold (30 minutes proposed), listing every branch still owed, and one notice when all is pushed again.\n- The reed status bar shows one field, `sync: \u003cminutes\u003e`: the minutes since this machine last had nothing owed to origin, so the operator sees before switching machines whether work is still only local.\n- Agents may still push, but a push of theirs that fails never stops them: their instructions say a failed push needs no action, since lym pushes their commits later, and no row or gate requires an agent's commits to be on origin.\n- lym pushes everything it owns through the one pusher with these rules: both branches of every pair, beam branches and the board. Branches the operator made outside lym are left alone.\n- How often lym pushes is config in seconds, adaptive: a short interval while pushes go through (60 s proposed), doubled after each failed push up to a ceiling (300 s to start with), and back to the short one after a push lands.\n  The reed field shows the interval in force beside the minutes since the last sync, such as `sync: 3m · 60s`.\n- A landing makes lym push at once, but a network outage never stops other work: other runs go on committing locally.\n  A landing that needs no GitHub call merges locally and hands its owed push to the pusher; one that needs GitHub, such as a pull request, waits for the network and retries, and never fails on a transient error.\n- A refusal that retrying cannot fix (rejected push, expired login, moved branch) stops at once with one notice.\n\n## Operator intent (2026-11-26)\n\nLoomYard stops too often because a push to origin fails, above all on VPN at work.\nlym and the agents only commit; lym pushes in the background when it suits and retries a failed push later, with fewer pushes and less traffic.\nA finished task is the exception: its landing is pushed at once, so a milestone never stays only local.\n\n## What `async-run-pusher` (`b4236816a`) already built\n\nRun transitions commit locally, and a per-worktree pusher started by each commit, the dobby's tick and a resume pushes both sides, retries owed pushes with backoff and posts one notice after a minute.\nRead first what each part below still needs on top of it:\n`lym fabric commit`'s and `lym board sync`'s own detached pushes, the synchronous landing pushes and GitHub reads, and Seed-IDE's check of the code tip on origin.\nA part the pusher already settles is named as such in the change description and dropped.\n\nBundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# Periodic async push of pairs whatever made the commits\n\nImported from [issue #247](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/247).\n\n## Idea\n\nAgents should only have to commit; they should never have to think about pushing.\nlym takes care of every push itself, asynchronously, without blocking an agent.\n\n## Seen\n\nlym already pushes asynchronously after `lym fabric commit`, but only then.\nAn agent that commits with plain `git commit` leaves its commits unpushed until some later lym step pushes the pair, and the push failures that follow land on whichever step happens to push next.\n\n## Proposal\n\nA periodic pusher in lym, for example in the dobby or its own small daemon, that pushes each live pair's code and records branches when they are ahead of origin, however the commits were made.\n\n- Periodic and not aggressive: a fixed interval of minutes, not a push per commit.\n- Never blocks an agent or a row; a failed push is retried at the next period, with backoff, and reported once rather than per attempt.\n- Rows that need origin current, such as Finalize, still push synchronously as now.\n\n\n# Transient push failures are mailed to the orch one by one instead of retried\n\nImported from [issue #238](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/238).\n\nOn a network that resets HTTPS connections now and then (git exits 128 with 'Recv failure: Connection reset by peer'), every failed background push sends the orch a creel message naming `lym fabric push` or `lym board sync` as the way forward. In one hour on one hub the orch got eight such messages, for the bolt branch main, task code branches and records branches, and each retry by hand succeeded at once or failed again only because the connection was still flaky.\n\nExpected: fabric retries a transport failure itself with backoff, and mails the orch only once retries are exhausted. One message then covers every branch still unpushed, rather than one message per push. The next pair push already carries a withheld code side, so a single summary would suffice.\n\nRelated: #193 (closed), which made runs retry transient fetch failures; pushes have no such retry.\n\n# Unreachable origin halts the run as failed repeatedly instead of one retryable stop\n\nImported from [issue #243](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/243).\n\nAn unreachable origin halted the run as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure` seven times in a row, across PR-Review and Finalize, instead of waiting or blocking once.\n\nWhat happened:\n- PR-Review and Finalize halted with `landingshed: ... push the task branch ... fabricengine: ls-remote of \u003ctask\u003e-weft ... Recv failure: Connection reset by peer`.\n- Finalize then halted three more times with `landingshed: Finalize: read who owns the run: board: origin is unreachable, so the owner write was not pushed: fetching origin for the board ... failed (gitrepo: ... git exited 128 ...)`.\n- One halt in between was `blocked` with anomaly `escalation-to-human`: `owner check: origin was unreachable, so who owns this run could not be judged; re-run \"lym loom resume\" in the task worktree once origin answers`.\n- Each halt wrote a note and a history entry; the history shows `Finalize / stuck` then `Finalize / goto` before each re-failure.\n\nWhy it is abnormal:\n- A transient network failure is classed as a hard failure, the same class as a producer defect, and the run state becomes `failed`.\n- The same condition surfaced under three different halt shapes (`failed`, `blocked`, `failed`), so the operator cannot tell one outage from three problems.\n- Each re-step re-ran immediately and failed again within about a minute, so the retries consumed attempts without waiting for the remote.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Classify unreachable-origin errors (ls-remote, fetch exit 128, connection reset) as one retryable or `blocked` kind with a single note, and have the driver wait or back off before re-stepping.\n- Keep `failed` for errors that retrying cannot fix.\n\nProvenance: task `retire-d9`; notes loom-halt-3.md, loom-halt-4.md, loom-halt-5.md, loom-halt-6.md, loom-halt-7.md, loom-halt-8.md, loom-halt-9.md.\n\n\n# Finalize halts as hard failure on a GitHub API EOF that lym marks transient\n\nImported from [issue #240](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/240).\n\nA `lym loom step` at the Finalize producer read a pull request's status from the GitHub API and got `unexpected EOF`.\nThe step was refused with `transient=github-api`, yet the run halted as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure`.\n\nQuoted from the trace:\n\n```\nlevel=WARN msg=\"shed: step refused\" kind=producer transient=github-api error=\"landingshed: Finalize: read the pull request's status: pullrequest: read pull request #54: Get \\\"https://api.github.com/repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/pulls/54\\\": unexpected EOF\"\n```\n\nHalt note text: `loom halted failed at Finalize`, `state: failed`, `anomaly: producer-hard-failure`, 15 history entries, all earlier steps done or skipped.\n\nThis is abnormal because lym itself labels the error transient, and a single dropped connection to the API then ends a run that had finished every earlier step.\nOther network failures in the same run (an unreachable origin before a push) were retried until they landed, so the Finalize read is inconsistent with them.\n\nSuggested fix: when a step is refused with a non-empty `transient` kind, retry it with backoff, or leave the run resumable, instead of recording `producer-hard-failure`.\nA failure of this kind should reach `failed` only after the retry budget is spent.\n\nProvenance: task `transient-tripout-overpull`, from `loom-halt-2.md`.\n\n\n# Seed-IDE fails the run when the task branch's detached push has not landed yet\n\nImported from [issue #218](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/218).\n\nImport note: the issue names files under `.lym/`, which is deleted with its worktree, so only what the issue quotes of them reached the board: `.lym/logs`.\n\n## What happened\n\nRun `weekly-time-report` (recipe raddle, promoted from a Sketch) stopped at Seed-IDE with:\n\n\u003e run \"weekly-time-report\" cannot act: the code tip e7b0cc2 the run's last transition names is not on origin yet; way forward: run \"lym batten run weekly-time-report\" again once the code push lands\n\nTimeline from `.lym/logs` (2026-11-24, CET):\n\n- 14:45:52 Worktree-Create done; the task branch forks from the prime's local `main` at e7b0cc2, one commit ahead of `origin/main`.\n- 14:45:52 and 14:45:55 fabricengine spawned detached pushes of the new pair (`fabric --warp-path …/weekly-time-report …`).\n- 14:45:57 Seed-IDE's bootstrap checked whether e7b0cc2 is on origin, found it was not, and refused the step.\n- Shortly after, the detached push landed: `git ls-remote origin` showed `refs/heads/weekly-time-report` at e7b0cc2.\n\nThe network was slow and resetting that day (`Recv failure: Connection reset by peer` on records pushes), so the detached push took longer than the 2–5 s gap.\n\n## Effect\n\n- The run stopped as failed, and the tackler handed it back to the parent asking whether to push the prime's `main`, which was not needed.\n- A plain `lym batten run weekly-time-report` afterwards went straight through Seed-IDE to Run-Shed.\n\n## Expected\n\nA check that the code tip is on origin, right after lym itself spawned the push that puts it there, should wait for that push, or retry for a while, instead of failing the run.\nThe stop should also say that lym's own push of the task branch is in flight, so the tackler does not ask the parent to push the parent branch.\n\n\n# Finalize blocks on untracked leftovers of a project the merge-in deleted\n\nImported from [issue #242](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/242).\n\n## Seen\n\nA darn run's Finalize merged its parent beam into the task worktree after its pull request had merged on GitHub.\nThe beam had just taken in a main commit that deleted a whole C# project.\nThe task worktree still held that project's directory with only untracked, gitignored content: `bin/`, `obj/` and generated fixtures.\nOnce git removed the tracked files, the directory was no longer ignored by the deleted `.gitignore`, so it showed as untracked.\nFinalize then blocked: \"the task worktree has uncommitted changes after the merge-in: …/NORCE.Import.CommonLibHarness/; commit or remove them on the task branch, then resume\".\n\nThe run had no live agent left to remove it.\nThe orch may not edit a task worktree, and the Go driver only asked the parent a question and stopped again on resume.\nThe only ways forward were a goto back to the writer, which would re-run review and PR rows for a merged pull request, or the operator removing the directory by hand.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe operator removes the leftover directory by hand, then the orch runs `lym loom resume` in the task worktree.\n\n## Expected\n\nFinalize's merge-in treats a directory that holds only files the merge's base ignored, under a path the merge deleted, as leftover output:\nit removes it, or ignores it in the clean-tree check, and goes on.\nFailing that, the stop names a lym verb the parent can run to clear it, instead of needing a hand edit in the task worktree.\n\n\n\n# Seen 2026-11-26: a busy push lock fails a dobby start\n\nWith several tasks released at once, dobby's catch-up for `sandbox-closed-area-build` and `keep-landed-records` failed with `fabricengine: push lock busy: .../loomyard-weft/.weft/fabric.push.lock still held after 30s ... neither side was pushed`, and each needed a manual `lym batten run`.\nA held push lock is contention, not a failure: the catch-up should hand the owed push to the pusher and start the run.\n\n# Review findings carried over from `async-run-pusher` (`b4236816a`)\n\nCode-Review left these MEDIUM findings open; check each and close what is still open:\n\n- tests of the pusher's lock and start conditions (`internal/runpusher`);\n- tests of the pusher restart calls from the dobby tick and the batten and loom resumes (`internal/beamcli`, `internal/battencli`, `internal/loomcli`);\n- tests of the teardown's push and level wiring (`teardownPushSeams` in `internal/battencli/wire.go`);\n- Worktree-Teardown looping with pushes off (`internal/battenshed/teardown.go`).\n\n\n## From issue #254\n\nImported from [issue #254](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/254).\n\n## What happened\n\nA `lym shed` run halted `failed` with anomaly `interrupted-stop` at producer `Webster`.\nThe note says a machine stop likely ended the loop, which is not itself a defect.\nThe stderr it carries shows two things that are:\n\n1. The step wrote `status push is owed; retrying until it lands` for `producer=Drift-Expect` while origin was unreachable (`ls-remote` failed with `Connection reset by peer`).\n   It then logged `origin is unreachable before a push; pushing unchecked, so the push's own failure is recorded` twice, about 10 s apart.\n   The loop never produced an envelope, and the halt note was written only about 20 hours after the loop started, after the machine rebooted.\n   Nothing in the notes shows a bound on the retry, or any record of the push's own failure after the \"pushing unchecked\" lines.\n2. The note says step `Drift-Await` was in flight, while the stderr's producer is `Drift-Expect` and the history shows both `Drift-Await` and `Drift-Expect` as `skipped`.\n   The in-flight step name and the producer that owed the push disagree.\n\nQuoted lines (paths removed):\n\n```\nlevel=WARN msg=\"loomcli: status push is owed; retrying until it lands\" producer=Drift-Expect state=running error=\"fabricengine: push code side ...: ls-remote of \u003cbranch\u003e ...: exit 128: fatal: unable to access '\u003corigin\u003e': Recv failure: Connection reset by peer; way forward: run `lym fabric push` ... to retry the push\"\nlevel=WARN msg=\"loomcli: origin is unreachable before a push; pushing unchecked, so the push's own failure is recorded\"\nlevel=WARN msg=\"loomcli: origin is unreachable before a push; pushing unchecked, so the push's own failure is recorded\"\n```\n\n## Why it is abnormal\n\nA transient network failure on a status push should either land after a bounded retry or fail the step with a recorded push error.\nAn unbounded wait with no outcome stalls the whole run until something external kills it, and the halt then reads as a machine stop instead of a push problem.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Bound the \"push is owed\" retry, and record the push's final failure in the step's status and trace when it gives up.\n- Make the halt note's in-flight step and the producer owing the push name the same step, or say why they differ.\n\nProvenance: task `models-bit-rock-formation`, from `loom-halt-2.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:02Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:30:37Z","design_file":"design-push-retry-not-halt.md","decision_file":"","revision":"5f5c2f28bb79a479234dd2451ce82bde31550d83bbf5a7c4112ac3556f8288f4"},{"handle":"T17","slug":"keep-landed-run-records","title":"Keep each landed run's findings, asks and fixer reports in its archive tag, with an index row and lym report landed","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["landing","fabric","report","loom","burler","shed","cwd","git","testkit","pattern"],"status":"","project":"P6","section":"Layer C","waits_on":["T1","T9","T11","T12","T13"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `keep-landed-records`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record whole: the archive tags teardown pushes, the loom status commit and run-records set, the parent-review ask record's locked updates in internal/burlerengine, the landing fixer and fix review scratch reports, and Finalize's merge-in, merge and push steps\nDelivers: one kept directory under each run's _lym/ that the status commit stages and the archive tag carries, holding PR findings copies, ask record and report copies seeded back after an adopt, landing fixer and fix review report copies, the landing record and the index row; and a read-only lym report landed verb that lists and extracts every archived run's kept records\nExports: the kept directory's name in internal/lymdirs; the landing record and index row types with their writers and one reader in internal/shedrun; the first-parent landing searches in internal/fabricengine; pullrequest.API.Merge returning the merge commit SHA; the cobra verb lym report landed\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/lymdirs (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/loomengine (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/loomcli (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/fabricengine (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/burlerengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/landingshed (about 450 changed lines)\n- internal/pullrequest (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/pairteardown (about 5 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/prkit (about 5 changed lines)\n- internal/shedrun (about 140 changed lines)\n- internal/gitrepo (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/reportengine (about 430 changed lines)\n- internal/reportcli (about 170 changed lines)\n- PATTERN.md (about 4 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:21Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:03:14Z","design_file":"design-keep-landed-run-records.md","decision_file":"decision-keep-landed-run-records.md","revision":"cc25120d6c3722bfb86358a8c4865b5be4da37d8f99d729ef6aaa8d277ebd601"},{"handle":"T21","slug":"hardener-study-faults","title":"Faults the hardener study read in fabric, cleanup and teardown code","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["fabric","cleanup","batten"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Layer C","waits_on":["T11"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\n`docs/research/hardener-works-study.md` (`c6618b538`), section \"Faults found by this study\", read these in the code; none was driven:\n\n- a refused credential during a one-repository seed keeps the hub and says origin may still take the push;\n- an empty teardown job clears an earlier teardown's pending record;\n- the `cleanup` remote sweep counts local tags as landed and never pushes them;\n- `--reset` is advised without regard to unpushed work;\n- `fetchRunForCatchUp` reads no records layout;\n- a `lym fabric remove` pending record can outlive its pair (`cd8090733` fixed only the cancel's records, by choice).\n\n## Idea\n\nConfirm each against the code with a test that fails first, and fix the confirmed ones.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:25:44Z","revised":"2026-11-26T13:19:56Z","design_file":"design-hardener-study-faults.md","decision_file":"","revision":"4c83e495e63861948521625ec478100b1646fded645cf4949e4d1f95185666c1"},{"handle":"T7","slug":"area-creel-identity","title":"creel across an area: kernel-attached sender identity, the area registry and the hub directory","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["creel","cwd"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record, D8 whole and the pieces of D3 and D6 that D8 rests on; peerPID (internal/creelengine/peer_linux.go), daemon.resolve and resolveSender, serveHubCall (receive.go), the outbound worker's spawn closure (outbound.go attempt), hubregistry's Entry, Lookup, Live and RepositoryName, creelcli's hubdirectory and its one machine-record write\nDelivers: every creel daemon sets SO_PASSCRED and SO_PASSPIDFD and takes the sender from SCM_CREDENTIALS and SCM_PIDFD with the pidfd liveness check; D3's inside test (barrier in /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/status or attr/current, plus a cgroup naming a scope of the hub's area by the whole-name forms lym-area-\u003chub key\u003e.scope and lym-area-\u003chub key\u003e-\u003cpane|run|attach\u003e-\u003cpid\u003e.scope), with which a daemon serves only deliver and confirm to a peer inside another hub's area or a peer gone or unreadable; the machine record moved to the hub directory \u003cper-user lym state dir\u003e/hubs/ with its lock, read under a shared lock on a read-only descriptor, with the old hubs.json read for entries the new record lacks; each daemon writing its shortname, creel socket path, repository name and the area-aware creel level into its entry, bar an in-area daemon; the area registry area-hubs.json and its lock in the hub directory, its reader, and a writer the core part's lym area start calls; hubregistry.Lookup, the confirm's geometry and RepositoryName reading an area hub's registry entry and, inside an area, every other hub's host-written record, never a Bolt shortname file or a prime's .git/config; every registry reader and writer dropping an entry whose area file ~/.config/lym/area-\u003chub key\u003e.yaml is gone (Added after Discussion); the outbound worker starting no other hub's daemon inside an area, and a hub without an area starting no area hub's daemon; PATTERN-agent-addressing's Across hubs background gaining the area-registry repository rule\nExports: areaengine.HubKey (reedengine.ServerName of the canonical hub path), areaengine.FilePath(hubKey), areaengine.ScopeName and the scope-name parser, areaengine.Inside(pid, hubKey, barrier); hubregistry.AreaEntry {hub_path, shortname, creel_socket, repository}, hubregistry.ReadAreaRegistry and hubregistry.WriteAreaEntry, the hub-directory path helper; the area-aware creel level constant D5 admits\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/creelengine (about 450 changed lines)\n- internal/hubregistry (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/creelcli (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/areaengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/lymdirs (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/slackcli (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-creel-identity.md","decision_file":"decision-area-creel-identity.md","revision":"4d56faff3481a8de1737a1276c5f4a0babbea1a7acc787ab152a3389b5c0712b"},{"handle":"T12","slug":"beam-member-parent","title":"A beam member's run names its beam orch as parent, which may amend the beam's record","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["batten","landing","orch","stencil","shed","loom","pawl"],"status":"draft","project":"P5","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T9","T11"],"brief":"","body":"Bundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# A beam member's run records the prime orch as its parent orch\n\nImported from [issue #232](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/232).\n\n## Seen\n\nIn the DrillOpPlan hub on 2026-11-24, the run `models-stick-slip-map`, a member of the beam `models-torque-drag` and spawned from the beam's worktree, records the prime's orch `dop:orch` as its parent orch.\nIts PR-Review sent the `[pr-review request]` for PR #52 (into the beam branch `hanf/models-torque-drag`) to `dop:orch`, as ask 1 from the PR-Review session and as ask 2 from `dop:LOOM`.\nThe beam's orch `dop:models-torque-drag:orch`, which owns and released the run, reviewed the PR, and its answer was refused:\n\n```\ncaller dop:models-torque-drag:orch is neither the run's parent orch nor the strand the ask went to\n```\n\nThe beam's dobby sends its notices for the same run to `dop:orch` too.\nKnatte18/loomyard#231 shows the same gap in the tackler and driver prompts of a beam member.\n\n## Expected\n\nA beam member's run records the beam's orch as its parent orch, so its parent-review asks, notices and escalations go to the beam's orch and that orch's answer is accepted.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe prime orch forwards each ask and notice to the beam's orch, and runs `lym loom parent-review answer \u003cslug\u003e --ask \u003cn\u003e --satisfied` in the task worktree on the beam orch's verdict.\n\n\n# Tackler and driver prompts of a beam member say no parent is recorded\n\nImported from [issue #231](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/231).\n\n## Seen\n\nIn the DrillOpPlan hub on 2026-11-10, the shed tackler of run `models-backend`, a member of the beam `adopt-models-sim`, got a prompt whose section \"Your parent\" read:\n\n```\nNo parent is recorded for this run.\nWhen you are blocked, write your report and stop.\n```\n\nThe run had a parent: the beam's orch `dop:adopt-models-sim:orch`, which owned and released it.\nOther tackler and driver prompts in the hub carried the same text, in the form \"No parent is recorded for the run, so the question is written for whoever owns it.\"\nThe tackler escalated on a merge conflict and stopped; the prime orch saw the stop and passed it to the beam orch by hand.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe tackler and driver prompts of a beam member name the beam's orch as the parent, so a blocked tackler or driver reaches it directly.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe prime orch forwards a beam member's stop to the beam's orch by creel.\n\n\n# Beam orch cannot amend its own beam's decision record\n\nImported from [issue #236](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/236).\n\n## What happened\n\nIn hub `dop`, beam `models-torque-drag` (P1) holds a stored decision record.\nOn 2026-11-24 the operator made a decision that supersedes two of its decisions: a new member task maps the well's formation strength into the Models bit-rock law, where the beam record's D1 and D2 keep Models' bit-rock defaults.\nThe member's Discussion saw the conflict and asked the beam orch, which owns the beam record, to settle it.\n\nThe beam orch could not amend its own beam's record:\n\n- `lym loom decision add models-torque-drag --by parent ...` from the beam orch refused: `the caller \"dop:models-torque-drag:orch\" is a task session; way forward: a design call after the Discussion is the parent's to record; ask dop:orch to run the verb`.\n  The beam orch is an orch, not a task session, and it is the record's owner.\n- `lym board upsert --record-file` refuses an entry a run holds, and a beam's project is always held by its pawl run (`running · Beam-Await`), so that way is closed for the beam's whole life.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe beam's own orch can append a decision to, or replace, its beam's decision record while the beam lives, as a run's parent can for a task's record.\nThe caller check counts a beam orch (`\u003chub\u003e:\u003cbeam\u003e:orch`) as an orch, not as a task session.\n\n## Related\n\nThe same misclassification of the beam orch shows in parent-review: `lym loom parent-review answer` refuses the beam orch for its member's pull request (`neither the run's parent orch nor the strand the ask went to`), filed by the hub orch as Knatte18/loomyard#232.\n\n\n\n\n## From issue #252\n\nImported from [issue #252](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/252).\n\nA beam orch received a PR-Review pause notice for a task of its beam, naming 'lym tender resume \u003cslug\u003e' as the way forward. Run from the beam orch's session, 'lym tender resume \u003cslug\u003e' was refused: 'pause: an agent may pause, resume or revive only its own pair; way forward: ask your parent session'. The beam orch is the task's parent. Workaround: the prime orch ran the resume. Expected: the parent orch of a task, beam orch included, may resume its tasks' tender pauses, or the notice names a way forward open to it.\n\n## From issue #251\n\nImported from [issue #251](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/251).\n\nA task's PR-Review agent paused its own pair with lym tender pause before a push. The beam orch, which parents that task, then could not lift the pause: lym tender resume \u003ctask-slug\u003e from the beam orch's session is refused with 'an agent may pause, resume or revive only its own pair'. Mail from the beam orch to the paused agent is not delivered while the pause holds, so the run stays stuck until the prime orch runs the resume.\n\nExpected: the orch that parents a run may resume that run's pair, as it is the session that settles the run's halts.","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:42Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:30:49Z","design_file":"design-beam-member-parent.md","decision_file":"","revision":"caa2fa3cbeb41d12c1a0bcf886320ddeb48ce4f7ad228bb57134239950fb8c04"},{"handle":"T13","slug":"scope-and-earlier-checks","title":"Close a task's scope at Code-Review, and catch earlier what a PR review found","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["loom","burler"],"status":"draft","project":"P5","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T9","T10"],"brief":"","body":"This task builds the scope note and items 3 to 6 of the Describe-text note's Idea; items 1 and 2 landed with `describe-review-and-sync`.\n\nBundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# Close the scope of a task when its first Code-Review round starts, and have lym hold the line\n\nImported from [issue #226](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/226).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nA task's scope can still change late.\nIn the C# hubs a parent orch recorded decisions that widened a task while the run was at PR-Review, after Code-Review had finished, and the reviewers never judged the diff against the widened record.\nIn one run Code-Review stopped at its round cap because a decision finding spawns no fixer, so the widening was in the record but never built.\n\nThe operator's rule: decisions go in as early as possible, and once the Code-Review perch has begun it is too late.\nThe last Code-Review round is the last link that can change anything.\nWhat comes up after that becomes a note for a new task, not a change to this one.\n\n## Idea\n\nClose a task's scope when its first Code-Review round starts, and have lym hold the line, so it is not only a rule for the parent orch.\n\n- Before the line, a decision enters the record as an `Added after Discussion` entry, and `code-scope` and the plan lenses read it.\n- After the line, no decision enters the record.\n  `lym loom decision add` (and `replace`) refuse once the run stands at or past the first Code-Review round, and the refusal names the way forward: file the call as a note for a new task.\n  The refusal reaches the parent and the agent the way the other `decision add` refusals do.\n- Two things stay outside the line: feedback from a person through the PR agent, and the mechanical fixes Finalize makes to a merge or test failure.\n- The orch procedure and the parent's prompt say the same, so the parent files a note instead of trying.\n- Read first how `lym loom decision add` decides where a run stands (`internal/loomcli/decision.go` already refuses at some stages), where the Drift rows sit against the Code-Review perch, whether a Drift verdict can send a run back past the line, and how darn, tapestry and arras recipes place their Code-Review perch.\n- Related issues: taking the parent out of PR-Review, and reviewing the Describe text and catching the rest of the parent's findings earlier.\n\n\n# Review the Describe text inside the Describe row, and catch the rest of what the parent finds at PR-Review earlier\n\nImported from [issue #225](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/225).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nIn the C# repos on one machine, a parent asked to review a PR always finds something.\nThat points to a design flaw: if the parent finds something, an earlier quality check missed it.\n\nThe operator's guess was that the delivered code often does not cover everything the Discussion's decision record asked for.\nThe operator judges the finding below, that most of it is the PR text, as not very important, and that it is solved by reviewing the Describe text too.\n\n## What the parent finds (analysis, 2026-11-24)\n\nAn analysis read 25 PR-Review rounds with findings, across four hubs; one more hub had no PR-Review records.\nAbout 65 findings are itemised in readable text; about 29 more were in scratchpad files that no longer exist.\nThe task worktrees and the Sketches of the landed tasks are gone, so the only full source is each hub's creel mail journal, and the gate attributions are mostly inferred.\nThe full report is a local file and is not committed.\n\n- About half of the findings (32 of 65) are about the PR description, not the code:\n  internal process or tool names in the text (12), wrong or overreaching claims (12), and content the task asked for that the text omits (8).\n- The rest are tests missing or too costly (10), wrong behaviour (8), doc or comment drift (7) and scope the Sketch did not hold (8).\n- The operator's guess holds only in part: 4 of 25 rounds cite a Sketch or task requirement that was missed, and all four concern the PR text or scope, not code behaviour.\n- A finding is often sent twice because the agent edits the summary file but not the GitHub PR body (about 5 rounds).\n\n## Idea\n\nThe Describe row writes the change description, which becomes the PR title, the PR body and the landing commit message.\nIt is a seat table (`DescribeTable` in `internal/landingshed/describe.go`) whose session already reads the decision record, and its only gate is mechanical (`ValidateDescription`); no agent reviews what the text says.\n\n1. Review the Describe text inside the Describe row with a second agent, an assistant seat, and send its findings back to the writer; Describe is the last step that uses an LLM, so no review runs after it.\n   The reviewer checks that the text names no internal process or tool (board, task, scoper, Sketch, decision record, \"item N of\"), that every claim holds against the diff and the task's report or measurements, and that every deliverable the Sketch says to state in the description is there.\n   The mechanical gate stays in front of it.\n   This covers the 32 findings about the text.\n2. Sync the GitHub PR body from the description on every push, and verify the two match (about 5 re-sent rounds).\n3. Run the mutation or redundancy tier on every C# task, and give the Plan a tier0 cost budget (10 test findings).\n4. Have Discussion record what the parent will check: measured figures, doc statements and test intent.\n5. Let a Code-Review finding the parent already ruled in scope reach a fixer; today a decision finding spawns none.\n6. Re-check a constant quoted in a doc when the case it was measured on changes.\n\nThe issue on taking the parent out of PR-Review leaves these checks to the cold-start reviewers, so they must run before PR-Review, and a task's scope closes at its first Code-Review round (see the issue on closing a task's scope).\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:14Z","revised":"2026-11-26T08:20:23Z","design_file":"design-scope-and-earlier-checks.md","decision_file":"","revision":"24677fdc486f8bdaa132c49679e27e98b40ad33e3081a19c281b0f8a8b3298fd"},{"handle":"T14","slug":"area-seccomp-core","title":"Stage 1 of the closed area: supervisor, Landlock ruleset, connect filter, attach client and the in-area engines, token and relay","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["reed","shuttle","claude","codex","creel","dobby","slack","process","cwd","git","cli","docs","pattern"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T7","T10","T8","T11"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the creel identity part's exports (inside test, scope names, area registry writer, hub directory, area-aware level); config-keys-nested's rewritten PATTERN-config-layers line; docs/research/platform/seccomp-connect-probe/main.go as D4's starting point; reedengine's server spawn and scrub (lifecycle.go), reedcli's attach (EnsureSession, Status, the watchdog spawn), proc.CapProcessMemory and RunMemoryCapped, lymtemp's root and run directories, runproc's end verbs, claudeengine's account resolution, TranscriptReader and user-setup source, codexengine's homes and links, githubclient's token chain, slackengine's EnsureRelay, StopRelay and file geometry, creelcli's SendReady\nDelivers: lym area init, start, stop, show, supervise, exec, git-credential and slack-setup; the area file outside the hub and its write-grant refusals; the supervisor in its own scope with its record, socket, SO_PEERCRED check, subreaping and verbs boot, attach-scope, cap-memory, kill-scope and slack-relay (start, stop, presence, status); the one launcher setting no_new_privs, Landlock ABI 8 with TSYNC and the filter with TSYNC, TSYNC_ESRCH and NEW_LISTENER; the connect decision on the checked inode and the derived allow list; the per-hub area root under lymtemp; the host-side attach client under the area; lym area start writing the machine record and the registry entry for an area hub; the host-side refusal of D7 as the first root pre-run step and at every hub selection, with machine walks skipping area hubs; dobby and creel spawn sites refusing from outside; the memory caps through the supervisor; the area's own engine config directories, GitHub token and relay records; README.md, docs/overview.md and the glossary per D16, the pseudo-terminal bound included (Added after Discussion); PATTERN-closed-area added, PATTERN-child-processes changed, and the backgrounds of PATTERN-child-processes, PATTERN-lym-files and PATTERN-config-layers per the notes for the plan writer; the seccomp half of the under-each-barrier criteria, whose apparmor half the fallback part carries; and the D6 attach criterion's second clause, which the record's unindented continuation line cuts from the parsed bullet: a `detach-client -E` sent to the attach client runs its command under the area's filter and in the attach scope\nExports: the supervisor's verb protocol and its client in internal/areaengine (one request per connection, the verbs named above), the launcher's start-request protocol, the area file type and its loader, the barrier enum seccomp and apparmor, and the start check's hook for a barrier-specific refusal, on which the fallback and host-exit parts add verbs and a barrier\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 3000 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 500 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/reedengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 400 changed lines)\n- internal/proc (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/lymtemp (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/runproc (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/creelcli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/hubregistry (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine/claudeengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine/codexengine (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/githubclient (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/slackengine (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/slackcli (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/hubgeom (about 80 changed lines)\n- README.md (about 80 changed lines)\n- docs (about 80 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 40 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:03:20Z","design_file":"design-area-seccomp-core.md","decision_file":"decision-area-seccomp-core.md","revision":"22682274a3c1633ab72a1bfa268a659744f0d30eee5f5e9862bde663ea7b497f"},{"handle":"T15","slug":"area-apparmor-barrier","title":"The closed area's AppArmor fallback barrier, built only when the OSL-1046 probe fails","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["process"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T14"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the core part's supervisor, launcher, attach client, area file with its barrier key, and the start check's barrier hook; the OSL-1046 probe result of D14 step 1\nDelivers: lym area profile rendering the hub's profile from the area file's lists and the Landlock paths, with other hubs' creel sockets as one creel-*.sock pattern and the name a hash of the text, and naming the root load command; the launcher and attach client entering it through aa-exec, each still installing a filter with D4's foreign-ABI, io_uring_setup and clone3 rules, no connect rule, no listener, and TSYNC with TSYNC_ESRCH (Added after Discussion); the start refusing while no loaded profile carries the rendered name; the inside test's apparmor label branch exercised end to end\nExports: areaengine's apparmor barrier and its profile renderer\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 100 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-apparmor-barrier.md","decision_file":"decision-area-apparmor-barrier.md","revision":"2cd8c22abd358be34dc337618b3e0a9099c807378e35e79a040f22ae5bd411ac"},{"handle":"T16","slug":"area-host-exits","title":"Stage 2 of the closed area: window, hibernate and deploy as supervisor verbs, and lym ide refused inside","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["termwindow","hibernate","batten","ide","deploy","reed"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T15"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the core part's supervisor verb protocol and attach client; termwindow's Launcher.OpenAttach, hibernatecli's request and its systemctl spawn, battenshed's LandDeps.RunDeploy and its wiring, idecli; the operator's stop of D14 step 3\nDelivers: the supervisor's window verb (direct child of the hub root, title built from the directory name, Konsole running the host's lym reed attach with no shell after it) with lym reed window routed to it inside an area; the hibernate verb running systemctl hibernate alone; the deploy verb recording a pending deploy from Batten's Land row with a notice; lym area deploy cloning https://github.com/\u003crepository\u003e.git into the area state directory, fetching the area file's deploy_branch, checking the commit is on it and running the area file's deploy command with the host's toolchain and caches, refusing with the way forward when repository, deploy or deploy_branch is missing, and lym area init writing deploy_branch as main (Added after Discussion, with its acceptance criterion that a rewritten origin and a hub record naming another branch change neither); lym ide refusing inside an area\nExports: the window, hibernate and deploy verbs and their clients\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/termwindow (about 50 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/hibernatecli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/battenshed (about 60 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/idecli (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-host-exits.md","decision_file":"decision-area-host-exits.md","revision":"a3b15d5602b78c496bf04602933ffdee0cea5c686924e38df257f3c0e44152c4"},{"handle":"E1","slug":"hardener-recipe-changes","title":"Hardener rounds raise what they drive and converge on one rule","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["burler","stencil"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\n`docs/research/hardener-works-study.md` (`c6618b538`) finds that the hardener finds real faults and fixes them with tests that hold, but does not converge on a sound basis:\nboth campaigns ended on rounds that could not raise what they saw or had not driven what they claimed, and several fixes brought new faults no round caught.\n\n## Idea\n\nThe study's \"Changes to consider\":\n\n1. Exempt a hardener round's findings from the changed-file and changed-hunk rules in `burler-review-format.md` and `burlerengine.CheckReviewRound`, not only `origin: focus` ones.\n2. Name FAIL beside WARN in `harden-rubric-review.md`, so a failed focus entry always blocks.\n3. Make the rubric and the Bouncer state one convergence rule for a MEDIUM first raised in the closing round.\n4. Add a kill rule to the rubric: kill each job from each caller, once with origin applying the in-flight push and once refusing it, then type the printed command in the state left.\n5. A prior finding with an undriven half stays `open`; `resolved` needs a whole repro.\n6. The fix half drives the neighbours of its fix: the other failure kinds a new classifier receives, and every role that reads a new way forward.\n7. The verdict lists what no sandbox can drive (owner takeover, the pull-request sweep, a lost lease) as filed follow-ups.\n8. Run the next campaign with one opus round and compare it with the `sonnet[high]` rounds before keeping the default.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T11:25:43Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:25:44Z","design_file":"design-hardener-recipe-changes.md","decision_file":"","revision":"037754ac7ce304755d0c2132e6fd4b7a9276508074af4b4cd301c37f8e4b4d71"},{"handle":"E2","slug":"bridge-inbound-per-orch","title":"Cross-machine mail into the receiving orch own channel instead of a channel per orch pair","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["slack","creel"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-26, relayed by norce:orch on osl46)\n\nThe first cross-machine test over the Slack bridge worked both ways (Neve #2650, osl46 #129).\nSetup was clumsy: `approve \u003cmachine\u003e` by name failed, and each side needed `approve \u003cmachine\u003e \u003capp id\u003e`.\nBridge channels are made per pair of orchs, so their number grows with the square of the orchs and machines.\n\n## Idea\n\nOne inbound channel per orch: its existing channel.\nAn orch on another machine posts its mail into the receiver's channel.\nThe receiving relay checks the sender bot's app id against the approved peers, as today.\nChannels then grow linearly.\n\nCost and open points:\n\n- A conversation between A and B is split across two channels.\n  A could mirror its outgoing mail into its own channel, or the two could use threads, so the operator sees the whole exchange in one place.\n- The relay must keep telling the operator's messages apart from peer bots' posts in the same channel.\n- Why approve by name found no bot `lym-neve` is unexplained; a design that still names peers must find them.\n\nThe operator will think further about it; this is a note, not a task.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T13:15:12Z","revised":"2026-11-26T13:15:12Z","design_file":"design-bridge-inbound-per-orch.md","decision_file":"","revision":"b1841901bba9e4b49544c1661a0e4f80a7cedb813c986ed3bc02f4c210714e15"},{"handle":"E3","slug":"config-key-comment-sweep","title":"Name config keys as \u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e in Go comments","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["docs"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"After `config-keys-nested` nests the config keys by area, about 525 Go comment lines in about 199 files, plus single-word keys on top, still name a template key bare.\nPATTERN-config-key-names asks that such a comment say `\u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e`.\n\nSweep every Go comment and package doc that names a config key to its nested `\u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e` form.\nA comment-only change, no behaviour or user-visible text.\nDeferred from T3 code review round 1, finding F1, to keep that run short.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T13:52:00Z","revised":"2026-11-26T13:52:00Z","design_file":"design-config-key-comment-sweep.md","decision_file":"","revision":"7a81428b2f770a1b74172247671c848f8060382e9b42790148bf166c85c25d28"},{"handle":"F17","slug":"orchestrator-survey-ideas","title":"Remaining leads from the survey of comparable agent orchestrators (Oct 2026)","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["burler","shed","orch","quarry","report"],"status":"","project":"P2","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Leads borrowed from comparable open-source agent orchestrators, surveyed in October 2026 (first imported from issue #671).\nNothing here is a commitment; these are leads to triage.\nThe orch checked each against the code on 2026-11-17: ideas lym has built or that proved moot are dropped,\npartly built ones name only what is still open, and the eight most worth doing are notes of their own in this project.\nEach bullet names its source project and the lym module it would touch.\n\n## Plan validation and concurrency (quarry, Plan-Write, board)\n\n- **Typed scope operations on cards** (foremerge): each scope carries an additive or breaking operation,\n  and deterministic rules flag colliding pairs even when files do not overlap.\n  Worth comparing against board labels and the alignment gate.\n- **Symbol leases between concurrent tasks** (Fusion, ORCH): expiring leases on symbol keys serialize overlapping tasks,\n  with a deterministic tie-break; lym keeps overlapping tasks apart by board labels.\n- **Commit-time reservation guard** (ntm): a pre-commit hook refuses a commit touching what another task holds; needs leases first.\n- **Criterion amendment ledger** (oh-my-claudecode): a card's acceptance text changes only through a recorded amendment in weft.\n\n## Review (burler, Bouncer)\n\n- **Quorum of differently-angled judges** (ccswarm): lym fans out lenses but has no quorum vote for high-risk segments.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's parent orch can be asked to see and veto each Code-Review round and each drift judge ruling\n  (`code_review_ask_parent`, `judge_ask_parent`); that is one extra judge, not a quorum.\n- **Risk tier by changed paths** (yylo): globs over sensitive paths choose the review depth and the gate scope.\n\n## Engine, recipes and governance (shed, tender, orch, board)\n\n- **Cross-run quarantine** (Bernstein): a task that fails three times across runs is held, unless the failures were the host's.\n- **Fork a run from a step with a tree snapshot** (Bernstein): `shed goto` moves the cursor but does not restore the warp tree.\n- **Warm retry guarded by a workspace hash** (Bernstein): resume the native session only when the tree is unchanged.\n  Partly met since `dfade5f9a`: a run agent whose session a crash, reboot or killed pane ended resumes it (engines with a `SessionResumer`, so not Codex);\n  no tree hash guards that resume, and a retry after a timeout or a finished run still starts fresh.\n- **Evidence reuse by input identity** (yylo): verify is skipped on the same tree; a changed review prompt could rerun only the review.\n- **Guidance at the next step boundary** (Conductor): a queue of operator guidance for the next producer's prompt.\n  Partly met since 2026-11-19: `notify_parent_on` in `loom.yaml` mails the run's parent when a named row is reached,\n  so the parent can brief that row's agent as it starts; there is still no queue of guidance lym itself folds into the prompt.\n- **Batched operator questions with recorded assumptions** (Spec Kit `/clarify`, Conductor): Discussion-Write asks at most a few ranked questions,\n  each with a recommended answer, and records unanswered ones as assumptions.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's autonomous agents put their questions to the run's parent orch, not to the operator in their pane.\n  Discussion asks in its pane only when `discussion_interactive` is `true`, which is off by default and forced off by autonomy mode (`30ed1ee58`),\n  so this lead concerns an interactive Discussion only.\n- **Rate-limit wait**: the orch watcher wakes the orch after a usage reset; tender itself schedules no resume.\n- **Standing goals and scheduled routines** (ORCH, CAO, Fusion): periodic triage, dependency bumps or `selfreport` sweeps.\n\n## Agent sessions (reed, shuttle)\n\n- **Waiting-for-input signal** (agent-of-empires): shuttle has an awaiting-input state; screen heuristics still decide readiness and startup.\n- **Reference status patterns and a paste pitfall** (CAO): compare its regexes and its tmux bracketed-paste note with shuttle's readiness and creel inject.\n- **Context forecasting** (ntm): the orch cycles its context; Webster's Master and the loom driver do not, and nothing forecasts.\n- **Staggered spawns** (ntm): space Webster forks and batten fan-out by learned rate limits.\n\n## Observability (report, weft)\n\n- **Hash-chained run journal** (Bernstein): a hash chain over producer outputs makes an archived run self-verifying.\n- **Unverified-completion rate** (Bernstein): a `lym report` view of completions with no test or gate behind them.\n- **Commit-to-record links** (AgentNote): weft links to warp by a `Warp-SHA:` trailer; a note or trailer on each warp commit could link back.\n- **Run diff** (ccswarm): `lym report` comparing two archived runs' timelines and token use.\n- **OpenTelemetry spans per producer** (Conductor).\n\n## Operator attention (orch, dobby, reed)\n\n- **Attention feed with acknowledgement states** (ntm): dobby's wait reminders have no seen, snoozed or acknowledged states.\n\n## Discussion review (Discussion-Write, stencils, pattern)\n\n- **Requirements-quality checklist** (Spec Kit `/checklist`): items with IDs the Discussion-review judge answers one by one.\n- **Fixed analysis categories** (Kiro): inconsistency, ambiguity, conflicts, unstated assumptions and missing edge cases as named lenses.\n- **PATTERN rules handed to reviewers** (Spec Kit constitution): its rules as numbered MUSTs in plan and code review prompts.\n- **Glob-scoped steering** (Kiro): stencils pull per-area guidance chosen by the paths of a plan's glyphs.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-10-16T06:52:07Z","revised":"2026-11-26T08:01:36Z","design_file":"design-orchestrator-survey-ideas.md","decision_file":"","revision":"5943695b095748396e00d09d15155719e05150e135b3d238308638c01ea7242a"},{"handle":"R4","slug":"antigravity-engine-spike","title":"Antigravity CLI engine spike","kind":"note","types":["research"],"areas":["shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Research","waits_on":[],"brief":"Hands-on spike of agy in a tmux pane: screens, hooks, transcript, login","body":"## Seen (orch, 2026-11-17)\n\nThe desk study `docs/research/gemini-antigravity-engine-study-2026-11-17.md` (`246c9146`) judged Antigravity CLI (`agy`) the better Google engine target.\nIts terms aim at third-party services reselling access, not at the operator driving the CLI with a tool they wrote, as with Codex and Claude (operator, 2026-11-17).\nSeveral of its readings are undocumented and can only be learnt hands-on.\n\n## Idea\n\nA hands-on spike like Codex's of 2026-10-24, run as darn docs, that installs `agy` and runs it in a tmux pane on Neve.\nIt answers the study's open points:\n\n- the busy, idle, trust and interrupt screens, and bracketed paste plus Enter through `paste-buffer -p`;\n- whether the keyring login works in a lym pane;\n- the hook payloads for SessionStart, PreToolUse, PostToolUse, PostInvocation and Stop (`continue`, `fullyIdle`);\n- the transcript's on-disk layout and token fields;\n- whether a conversation id can be chosen up front, and whether the `self` subagent inherits the caller's history.\n\nCap: one machine, one model, one short session per point, no lym code changed.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","revised":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","design_file":"design-antigravity-engine-spike.md","decision_file":"","revision":"050ddc1b61f6b055aa9d9b6c628533fe44d2d55b1714f47bce2bcd427506c7b5"}]};
